{"id": "153292", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153292", "title": "Mathiveri", "text": "Mathiveri is a small island that belongs to North Ari atoll, Maldives. Only 900 people live there.\nGet in.\nMathiveri can be reached by slow public ferry from Mal\u00e9 on Su, Tu, Th at 09:00, trip takes 5 hours and costs 53 MVR. Timetable can be checked at the company operating these routes web site. This boat then proceeds to other islands of North Ari Atoll: Feridhoo (1 hour), Maalhos (1 hr 30 min) and Himandhoo (2 hours). In the opposite direction to Male it passes such islands as: Bodufulhadhoo (trip takes 30 min), Ukulhas (1 hour), Rasdhoo (2 hours).\nThree times weekly there is a public boat to Thoddoo, trip takes 3 hr 15 min, costs 21 MVR.\nFast boats are available too, trip lasts for two hours and costs US$50 (as of 2016). Companies operating on this route:\nConnect.\nMobile operators Dhiraagu and Ooredoo operate on the island and offer 3G/LTE type of connections.\nDhiraagu provide fastest home broadband to this Island.\nCope.\nThere is a health centre providing basic health care services and also a police station."}
{"id": "153293", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153293", "title": "Thoddoo", "text": "Thoddoo is an isolated inhabited island that administratively belongs to North Ari atoll, Maldives. It's quite large by Maldivian standards, it will take about an hour to circumvent it. In 2014, it had a population of about 1,500 people (including foreigners).\nUnderstand.\nThoddoo Island is surrounded by a wonderful beach. The locals are very friendly and very welcoming, but due to Muslim restrictions you can only swim at the designated tourist beach which sometimes is called the bikini beach. While outside the tourist beach it advisable to wear beach clothing rather than bikinis or swimming slips.\nThe film \"Ghosts Can\u2019t Do It\" was filmed the Island of Thoddoo. The cast stayed in tents on the beach.\nThis island is a farm island: mainly passion fruits and papayas are grown, and they are among the tastiest and freshest you can eat. Mangoes, watermelon, eggplants, zucchinis, tomatoes, lettuce are grown very limited.\nGet in.\nIsland can be reached by a fast boat from Mal\u00e9 everyday at 16:00, trip takes 1 hour and costs US$30-35 (as of Jan 2017). In the opposite direction to Male it leaves at 07:00. This schedule is not very reliable so it's better to check with your guesthouse in Thoddoo for the timing and pricing. Companies operating on this route:\nThree times weekly (Sa M W at 07:00) there is a public ferry to Rasdhoo, trip takes 1 hr 15 min, costs Rf 21. The boat then proceeds to Ukulhas, departing at 13:00, trip takes 55 min and costs Rf 21. And other islands of North Ari Atoll: Bodufulhadhoo (trip takes 1 hr 30 min from Rasdhoo), Mathiveri (2 hours), Feridhoo (3 hours), Maalhos (3 hr 30 min) and Himandhoo (4 hours).\nSee.\nYou can see the Fresh fruit farms\nBuy.\nLots of grocery shops with very extensive range of products, big variety of fruits and vegetables.\nThere is an ATM of Bank of Maldives near the ferry dock, which is the only ATM on the island. It also worth to note that any place on the island will readily accept US dollars as a payment, but not credit cards.\nThere are a few souvenir shops in the island.\nEat.\nSome guest houses allow you to use their kitchen.\nSleep.\nAbout twenty of accommodation options exist, most of them are very simple guesthouses. Price range is roughly US$50-80 (2016), it gets much cheaper during off season and doubles during peak season.\nConnect.\nBoth mobile operators (Dhiraagu and Ooredoo) operate on the island and offer LTE connection.\nGuesthouses tend to have WiFi with a very slow and sometimes nonexistent Internet. While restaurants either don't have WiFi or refuse to share their WiFi passwords with customers not staying at their hotel."}
{"id": "153301", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153301", "title": "Rhuddlan", "text": "Rhuddlan is a small village in Denbighshire, two miles inland from Rhyl on the north Wales coast. It's best known for the substantial ruin of its 13th-century castle.\nGet in.\nA525 between Rhyl and St Asaph, just north of the A55. Rhyl is the closest rail station."}
