{"id": "153579", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153579", "title": "Garachico", "text": "Garachico is a small town in Tenerife.\nUnderstand.\nGarachico (from Guanche \"gara\", or 'island', and Spanish \"chico\", or 'small') is named after the uninhabited rocky islet off the coast, which has become the emblem of the town. The area was settled in 1496 by Italian merchants, and was established as a town in 1500. Its economy was based on sugarcane and wine, and it quickly became the most important harbour on the north coast. Following a devastating fire in 1697, and the volcanic eruption in 1706 of \"Trevejo\", during which much of the city and its harbour were destroyed by lava flows, the city lost its importance.\nSome parts of the original town survived, while the rest was rebuilt on top of the new rock. Along the coast visitors can still clearly see the lava flows which reached to the sea. During the week the town is quiet, apart from the many visitors who arrive on tourist buses and enjoy inexpensive midday meals. On weekends things tend to be a bit livelier, usually with locals.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nHighway TF-42, which connects Icod de los Vinos with Buena Vista del Norte, passes through Garachico. From Masca and Santiago del Teide in the south, travellers can cross the Teno Mountains on highway TF-82, and in El Tanque pick up TF-421, which descends along hairpin curves to the coast.\nBy bus.\nTitsa[dead link] bus 363[dead link] connects the town with Icod de los Vinos (\u20ac1.45, 11 min) and with Puerto de la Cruz (\u20ac3.75, 55 min), leaving every 30-60 minutes.\nGet around.\nThe town is on a relatively flat part of the coast and is therefore easy to navigate by foot.\nThere several free car parks, with one along the coastal road across from a retaining wall, and a second on the western end of the city by the former harbour. If all spots are taken, there is another smaller a bit further along the road heading west.\nSleep.\nCamping.\nThe closest official camp grounds are up the hill south:"}
{"id": "153581", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153581", "title": "Bushmills", "text": "Bushmills is a village historically in County Antrim, near the coast 60 miles north of Belfast. Its two big attractions are the Old Bushmills Distillery within the village, and Giant's Causeway 3 miles northeast, so in summer it's very touristy. With a population in 2021 of 1234, Bushmills is now part of Causeway Coast and Glens District, as the counties are no longer units of government. \nThe local attractions reflect the basalt bedrock, which crops out in magnificent columns along the coast. Inland the basalt creates the distinct mineral content of St Columb's Rill, the stream that arises in the boggy hills 5 miles southeast, to flow into the River Bush at Bushmills. This once supplied five distilleries, which used peat from the bogs as fuel: the Old Distillery is their sole survivor.\nThe Visitor Information Centre on Main St is open Mar-Oct M-Sa 9AM-5PM. There's another at Giant's Causeway. \nGet in.\nTrains between Belfast and Derry stop at Coleraine, with a branch line to Portrush; change for the bus to Bushmills. \nUlsterbus 402 runs from Coleraine to Portrush, Dunluce (for castle), Portballintrae, Bushmills, Giant's Causeway, Ballintoy (for Carrick-a-Rede bridge) and Ballycastle. It runs every 30 min daily.\nBus 172 from Coleraine bypasses Portrush and heads straight to Bushmills, then follows the same route to Ballycastle. There are four M-F and two Sa Su. \nBy road from Belfast take M2 / A26 to Ballymoney then B62 / B17 north into the village. If the weather's fine and you've got all day, take the scenic coast road from Belfast via Carrickfergus, Larne and the Antrim Glens.\nDay-trips from Belfast (and even from Dublin) run to the distillery, Giant's Causeway, and the other major sights nearby. If you don't have your own transport this is a good option.\nGet around.\nBus 402 / 172 links all points of interest along the coast. They're also within cycling range or a long hike.\nMarch-Oct a shuttle bus plies between Bushmills and Giant's Causeway. It runs every 20 min from the free P&amp;R car park on Main St."}
{"id": "153582", "revid": "2164467", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153582", "title": "Castlerock", "text": "Castlerock is a seaside village in County Londonderry, with a population of 1200 in 2021. Its big attraction is Mussenden Temple perched on the cliffs. \nGet in.\nBy road from Belfast follow M2 / A26 to Coleraine then the coast road, 62 miles.\nTrains run hourly from Belfast Grand Central via Antrim (for International Airport), Ballymena and Coleraine, taking 90 min to Castlerock, then another 30 min to Londonderry. Change at Coleraine for trains to Ulster University and Portrush, and for buses along the Antrim coast to Bushmills, Giant's Causeway and Ballycastle.\n is in town centre near the beach. \nUlsterbus 134 runs M-Sa every couple of hours from Coleraine along the coast road to Castlerock (20 min), Magilligan crossroads, Aghanloo Cross and Limavady.\nGet around.\nEverything is within walking distance of the railway station.\nNo local taxi, it would have to come from Coleraine.\nConnect.\nAs of Sep 2026, Castlerock and its approach roads have 4G from Three and Vodafone, and 5G from EE and O2."}
{"id": "153584", "revid": "2250869", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153584", "title": "Coloma (California)", "text": "Coloma is a small town in El Dorado County in California's Gold Country region. It was home to Sutter's Mill, the site where the California Gold Rush began after gold was discovered in 1848.\nUnderstand.\nColoma is a town that will live in the history books for its role in the California Gold Rush. While the discovery of Gold at Sutter's Mill on January 24, 1848 was not the first gold discovery in the state, news of the discovery spread quickly and inspired people from around the world to seek their fortunes in California. While initially it was the few thousand settlers who were already in the state who rushed to the Sacramento Valley in search of gold, starting in late 1848 over 300,000 people migrated to California from the rest of the United States and abroad, leading to California achieving statehood just two short years later in 1850. During the Gold Rush it is estimated that miners extracted over 750,000 lbs of the precious metal, but while a few struck it rich mining for gold, to a large extent it was the shopkeepers and merchants like blue jeans maker Levi Strauss who truly became wealthy during this time.\nToday, with a permanent population of only a few hundred people, the town is considered something of a ghost town due to its many abandoned buildings, including the old jail. Nearly 70% of the former town is now included in the 576 acre Marshall Gold Discovery State Historic Park.\nDo.\nColoma is a popular destination for whitewater rafting trips on the American River, with several outfitters based out of the town."}
{"id": "153600", "revid": "2164467", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153600", "title": "Lisnaskea", "text": "Lisnaskea (\"Lios na Sc\u00e9ithe\", \"fort of the shield\") is a town historically in County Fermanagh, Northern Ireland, midway between Enniskillen and the border with the Republic. It's a small place, but with a population of 3000 in 2021, it's the second-largest town in the former county, and it's the main base for exploring Upper Lough Erne. Local government boundaries have changed so Lisnakea is now part of Fermanagh and Omagh District\nUnderstand.\nA few miles southwest of the village, waterways twist and turn through lakes and wetlands and islets. The UK border with the Republic of Ireland and the European Union twists and turns in a similar way, creating \"pene-enclaves\" \u2014 strips of land that can only be approached by traversing the other country, unless you flounder across fields and ditches. During \"The Troubles\" of 1970-1999 that made it bandit country. More importantly for the honest majority on both sides, from 1921 the border blighted travel and transport: a simple freight delivery or trip to town was a logistic challenge. So Lisnaskea is one of many places that depend upon the continuance of the 1998 peace accord.\nGet in.\nBy road from Belfast follow M1 then A4 towards Enniskillen, branching south at Maguiresbridge onto A34.\nBy public transport travel via Enniskillen, which has buses from Belfast via Dungannon, from Dublin city and airport via Cavan heading for Donegal, and from Ballina via Sligo, Manorhamilton and Belcoo. \nUlsterbus 95 runs from Enniskillen via Lisbellaw and Maguiresbridge to Lisnaskea (30 min), continuing southeast to Newtownbutler and across the border to Clones in County Monaghan. There are five M-F and three on Saturday. \n is the main bus stop in Lisnaskea. \nThe village is by Upper Lough Erne, which is connected to the entire Irish navigable waterway system, reaching all the way to Athlone, Limerick, Carlow, Waterford and Dublin. Expect some push-back if you plan to take a hired boat all that distance.\nGet around.\nYou need wheels to explore this scattered district. A bike would do.\n 1641 AD: \"At Lissenskeah they hanged, or otherwise killed, above 100 persons, most of them of the Scottish nation.\"\nConnect.\nAs of May 2026, Lisnaskea and its approach roads have 4G from EE, Three and Vodafone, and 5G from O2. \nYou might also get 4G from Eir, but the other Irish carriers don't reach this far north."}
{"id": "153601", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153601", "title": "Irvinestown", "text": "Irvinestown is a small town historically in County Fermanagh in Northern Ireland, which is nowadays part of the larger Fermanagh and Omagh District. In 2021 it had a population of 2300, so it's the largest settlement along the north shore of Lower Lough Erne. \nNearby Necarne Castle is a country park around a ruined mansion, and the town itself used to be called Necarne. (That name may derive from \"Na Caorthann\", the rowans, or \"Na Cairn\" the heap of stones.) In 1618 during the Plantations it passed to a fellow called Lowther, who called it Lowtherstown. In 1667 it was sold to the Irvine family, but not until early 19th century did the village adopt their name.\nGet in.\nIrvinestown is on A32 ten miles north of Enniskillen and 17 miles southwest of Omagh.\nFrom Belfast or Dublin take the bus to Enniskillen and change. Ulsterbus 194 (a-j) runs every hour or so from Enniskillen to South West Hospital, Ballinamallard and Irvinestown (30 min), continuing to Lisnarick and Kesh. One bus a day extends to Pettigo and Ballyshannon.\nLower Lough Erne to the west of town is linked by navigable waterways to the Shannon, thence all the way to Athlone, Limerick, Dublin and Waterford.\nGet around.\nYou need wheels in this scattered district. A bike would do.\nConnect.\nAs of May 2026, Irvinestown and its approach roads have 4G with all UK carriers. 5G has not reached this area."}
{"id": "153602", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153602", "title": "Portadown", "text": "Portadown is an industrial town historically in County Armagh in Northern Ireland, but since 2015 part of the Armagh-Banbridge-Craigavon District. Its population in 2021 was 33,000. Its eastern parts are called Craigavon for the new town that was only partially built there. \nUnderstand.\nStanding at the south tip of Lough Neagh, Portadown was traditionally a linen-processing town that became a transport hub. In 1741 the Newry Canal linked Lough Neagh, Portadown, and the sea below Newry. In the 19th century the Dublin-Belfast railway arrived - the line swings inland through Portadown to avoid the hills south of Belfast. In 1965 construction began for a new town, Craigavon, intended to stretch from Portadown to Lurgan. Some housing estates were built but the project flopped as investment and employment drained from the area, perhaps a blessing in disguise as the design was based on ugly Cumbernauld in Scotland. It also offended potential Catholic residents that the place was named after Lord Craigavon (1871-1940), first Prime Minister of Northern Ireland, who sought \"a Protestant parliament for a Protestant people\".\nThe sectarian rift deepened during \"The Troubles\", with a steel wall dividing embittered Catholic and Protestant sections of the community. One local flashpoint was the Drumcree conflict, about the rights of Orange Order parades along a particular route, as they had since 1807 and to continue doing so although these were now housing estates with a mostly Catholic population. That conflict was dampened by the 1998 Good Friday Agreement between Britain and the Republic of Ireland. The GFA has enabled Belfast and Londonderry to relaunch themselves as destinations, but Portadown in common with the rest of Armagh has not yet done so.\nGet in.\nPortadown is on the A3 Lurgan to Armagh road 30 miles from Belfast just south of the M1.\nTrains run every 30 min from Bangor via Belfast (several stations), Lisburn and Lurgan to Portadown, taking an hour from Belfast.\nThe Enterprise Train runs hourly from Dublin Connolly via Drogheda, Dundalk and Newry to Portadown, 90 min, and continues to Belfast. \n is northwest side of town centre.\nGoldliner Bus 251 runs hourly from Belfast via Lurgan to Portadown (40 min) and Armagh. One or two per day continue from Armagh across the border to Monaghan and Cavan.\nUlsterbus 551 runs hourly from Belfast via Lisburn, Moira and Lurgan to Craigavon, 1 hr 40 min; it doesn't reach Portadown. \nBus 75 runs every couple of hours between Dungannon and Portadown, taking just under an hour. Change at Dungannon for Omagh, Strabane and Cookstown. \nBus 63 takes an hour between Newry and Portadown. There are six M-F and four Saturday.\nBus 62 runs from Banbridge via Gifford to Portadown, taking 35 min. It runs M-F hourly and Sa every couple of hours.\nBuses make multiple stops through town: Church St is the main stop but there isn't a station.\nGet around.\nYou need wheels to reach Ardress House: Bus 67 Portadown-Dungannon goes that way but only twice a day.\nUlsterbus 46 / 47 shuttles between Portadown, Craigavon and Lurgan every 15 min.\nNational Cycleway 9 runs mostly on-road from Belfast to Lisburn, Craigavon and Portadown then off-road to Scarva and Newry. Route 94 circles Lough Neagh. Route 91 goes all the way cross-country to Armagh, Monaghan and Enniskillen.\nTaxis in town are AAA (+44 28 3839 9993), Portadown Taxis (+44 28 3833 1100), fonacab (+44 28 3833 3888), and AH if you need an 8-seater (+44 7927 960096).\n Annagh United play in the NIFL Championship, the second tier, at tiny BMG stadium (capacity 950).\nConnect.\nAs of June 2025, Portadown and its approach roads have 4G from EE, Three and Vodafone, and 5G from O2."}
{"id": "153603", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153603", "title": "Newtownabbey", "text": "Newtownabbey is a district just north of Belfast. It's short of tourist sights and facilities, and you'd only come here for business or to attend the university. \nUnderstand.\nPlaces called Newtown or Newton in the British Isles were often \"new\" around the 12th century when their abbey was created, but this one dates from 1958. The core of it was indeed the white abbey established at the end of the 12th century, a daughter house of Dryburgh Abbey in Scotland run by the Premonstratensian Order or White Canons. By the 19th century little was left of that, but the name lived on in The Abbey, an Italianate mansion designed and later lived in by the architect Charles Lanyon; in 1907 this became a TB hospital. Whiteabbey became industrial and commuter-residential as Belfast grew rapidly: appropriately, one major local industry was a bleach works. It was also a port for coal, until Belfast city harbour was improved and the small ports along the Lough were less used.\nAfter the Second World War, Britain's housing was in poor condition and there was a spate of re-building, much of it ugly. In 1958 Whiteabbey was combined with the villages of Whitewell, Whitehouse, Jordanstown, Monkstown, Carnmoney and Glengormley to create the town of Newtownabbey. However, building was by infill and it wasn't the sort of greenfield creation seen at, for example, Skelmersdale in England, Cumbernauld in Scotland or Craigavon in Northern Ireland. Nor did it use the mechanism of a \"New Town Development Commission\" superseding local government. So Newtownabbey's growth was more organic though not much prettier than those examples.\nThe town today has mixed industries such as light engineering and computing, and hosts a campus of Ulster University. In 2021 it had a population of 68,000, making it the third-largest settlement in Northern Ireland.\nGet in.\nTrains from Belfast Grand Central run every 30 min, taking 20 min to. They continue northeast to (for Ulster University), Carrickfergus and Larne. \nAn hourly train from Belfast runs to (with no stop at Whiteabbey) and continues northwest to Antrim, Ballymena, Ballymoney, Coleraine (for branch line to Portrush) and Derry.\nMetro Bus 1A runs every 30 min from Belfast Donegall Place along Antrim Road to Glengormley, then crosses the north end of town to Carnmoney, Ballyduff and Monkstown Avenue; Buses 1C - 1J ply variant routes through the housing estates.\nMetro Bus 2 C-D-E-H runs every 20 min from Donegall Place along Shore Rd to Yorkgate, Newtownabbey Courthouse and south end of town up Doagh Rd to Devenish Drive.\nUlsterbus 563 runs from Belfast Laganside via High St, Ulster University, Yorkgate, Skegonell and Greencastle to Whiteabbey and Greenisland, continuing to Carrickfergus and Kilroot. It's every 30 min M-Sa and every hour or two on Sunday.\nBelfast ferry terminal is 3 miles south of Newtownabbey on A2 / M2.\nGet around.\nThe Metro buses take you across this sprawling town.\nJock's Cabs are on +44 28 9090 9909.\n Not much accommodation here, try Belfast#Sleep or Carrickfergus. \nConnect.\nAs of March 2026, Newtownabbey and its approach roads have 5G from all UK carriers."}
{"id": "153615", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153615", "title": "Standing Rock", "text": "Standing Rock Indian Reservation straddles the border between North Dakota and South Dakota. It is the country's sixth largest reservation at 3,750 square miles (9,250\u00a0km2). Population was 8,217 as of the 2010 census, from various tribes of the Sioux.\nUnderstand.\nAs for most tribes, the history of the Sioux over the last few centuries is a sad and complex tale with some battles fought, several treaties made, more-or-less all of them broken by the whites, and Indian land repeatedly reduced. The Sioux on this reservation are descendants of those who defeated Custer at Little Bighorn and were massacred at Wounded Knee.\nSee Indigenous cultures of North America for more general discussion of native cultures.\nDAPL protests.\nIn 2016, Standing Rock made news in a big way: that year, plans to run the Dakota Access Pipeline (DAPL) near the reservation were met with massive protests against what was seen as both a threat to the local water supply and, according to tribal elders, a desecration of sacred burial grounds. While legal challenges to the pipeline slowly worked their way through the courts, the reservation became the site of several huge semi-permanent camps of occupying protesters who aimed to block the pipeline's construction. The local Sioux by no means stood alone: over 100 other tribes from across the U.S. and Canada, along with some in South America and even New Zealand Maori, declared their support and in some cases sent people to the camps. There were also many non-Indian allies involved, including a number of celebrities.\nWhile the Obama administration decided in its waning days against granting an easement for the pipeline, the protesters' victory was short-lived: almost immediately after his inauguration, Donald Trump directed the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers to reverse his predecessor's decision, and construction of the pipeline (and the dismantling of the protest camps) proceeded quickly thereafter. The first oil began flowing through the DAPL in June 2017.\nOrientation.\nThere are no actual cities within Standing Rock. The main settlements are Fort Yates, Cannonball and McLaughlin. Fort Yates is the administrative center with all the tribal offices and Sitting Bull College.\nGet in.\nA number of highways pass through the region. McLaughlin is at the intersection of US\u00a012 running roughly east-west and SD\u00a063 north-south. Crossing the border, SD\u00a063 becomes ND\u00a06 which leads to ND\u00a024. ND\u00a024 goes near Fort Yates and Cannonball then leads north toward Bismarck."}
{"id": "153621", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153621", "title": "Hawick", "text": "Hawick is a town in the Scottish Borders, midway along the historic route from Carlisle to Edinburgh, with a population of 10,630 in 2020. The main reason to come is to explore the hills and forests above the town.\nUnderstand.\nIt's pronounced \"hoick\", one syllable, like the act of kicking a rugby ball high into the air. Several townsfolk have proved adept at doing so but Hawick's best-loved contributor to rugby, Bill McLaren (1923-2010), found fame as a commentator after illness ended his playing career. The town was also the birthplace in 1940 of the yachtsman Chay Blyth. \n\"Hawick\" in Old English means an enclosed farm. It stands at the confluence of the River Teviot and Slitrig Water, which from the 17th century were harnessed to power textile mills. Its initial business was budget staples such as stockings, then the mills moved into linen and knitware, practical tweeds then high-end cashmere and merino. The term \"Tweed\" began here as a 19th-century typo: twill was \"tweel\" in Scots but a London merchant thought it was a trade name for Tweed valley produce, and the name stuck. Hawick survived the 20th-century decline of the UK textile industry, but then the luxury sector also succumbed, and the town lost major employers such as Pringle. Other Border towns have been boosted by the re-opening of the railway, becoming commuter towns for Edinburgh, but Hawick is a little too far out to benefit.\nIn October 2021 the town was flooded, but all amenities described on this page were back in business in 2022.\nGet in.\nHawick is on the A7, 43 miles north of Carlisle and 50 miles south of Edinburgh. From eastern and southern England the quickest route is A1 to Newcastle, A696 / A68 to the Scottish border on windy Carter Bar, then A6088 through Bonchester Bridge to Hawick. \nBorders Bus X95 runs every two hours daily from Edinburgh along A7 via Galashiels and Selkirk to Hawick, taking 2 hours. It's quicker to take the train from Edinburgh to Galashiels then change to the X95, which between Galashiels and Hawick runs every 30 min. The two-hourly service continues south along A7 beyond Hawick to Mosspaul, Langholm, Canonbie, Longtown and Carlisle, another 80 min. \nThe \"Waverley Railway\" used to run through Hawick to join the main line at Carlisle but was axed in 1969. The northern part was restored in 2015, linking Edinburgh, Galashiels and Tweedbank; there are no plans to restore the southern part.\nGet around.\nThe town is small and walkable. A7 now runs west of the river, and the historic route along High Street has become a one-way north to south B-road. And A7 is no longer the main route to Edinburgh: from northwest England you follow A74(M) far to the west. This means that Hawick still has plenty of local traffic but no longer suffers from through-traffic.\nTaxi operators include Dom's (+44 7807 800078), Teviot Taxis (+44 7743 443775) and A1 (\u260f).\nBuy.\nGrocery stores:\nConnect.\nAs of May 2022, the town has 4G from all UK carriers, but coverage of the approach highways is very patchy. 5G has not reached this area."}
{"id": "153622", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153622", "title": "Selkirk", "text": "For the small Canadian city, see Selkirk (Manitoba).\nSelkirk is a market town in the Scottish Borders, with a population in 2020 of 4540. Its main trade of textiles has gone, and it's becoming a commuter town for Edinburgh, linked by the re-opened railway through nearby Galashiels. The main reason to visit is to explore the hills of Ettrick Forest. \nGet in.\nSelkirk is on A7, the traditional road between Carlisle and Edinburgh. From east side of England follow A1 then A68 to Jedburgh and St Boswells, then go west on A699.\nBorders Bus X95 runs every two hours daily from Edinburgh along A7 via Galashiels to Selkirk, taking 1 hour 40 min. It's quicker to take the train from Edinburgh to Galashiels then change to the X95, which between Galashiels, Selkirk and Hawick runs every 30 min. The two-hourly service continues south along A7 beyond Hawick to Langholm, Canonbie, Longtown and Carlisle, another 1 hr 40.\nBus 73 also plies hourly between Galashiels and Selkirk, taking 20 min. \nThere isn't a bus station, the stop is at the town crossroads in Market Place.\nGet around.\nYou need a car if you want to explore the hills.\n \"Some hae meat and canna eat, And some wad eat that want it; But we hae meat and we can eat, Sae let the Lord be thankit.\"\n - The \"Selkirk Grace\" probably originated in Galloway, but became better known when Burns recited it before a dinner laid on by the Earl of Selkirk\nConnect.\nAs of May 2022, the town has 4G from all UK carriers. 5G has not reached this area."}
{"id": "153625", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153625", "title": "Torngat Mountains National Park", "text": "Torngat Mountains National Park is a Canadian national park on the Labrador Peninsula in Nunatsiavut, occupies the northernmost tip of Labrador, Canada.\nUnderstand.\nThe Torngat Mountains are named for the Inuktitut word \"torngait\", meaning \"place of spirits\". The largest and most remote national park in Atlantic Canada, Torngat covers 9,700 km\u00b2 (3,700 sq mi) of Arctic Cordillera from Cape Chidley south to Saglek Fjord.\nHistory.\nThis is Inuit country, as it has been for thousands of years. The national park was created as part of the \"Nunavik Inuit Land Claims Agreement\" which came into effect in 2008. Under the agreement, Inuit people (formerly called \"Eskimos\", a term that is offensive to many Inuit) can continue hunting, fishing and trapping within the park boundaries.\nLandscape.\nTorngat is the southernmost part of the Arctic Cordillera, a mountain range which extends northward across Baffin Island and Nunavut. Terrain is rugged, with mountains and fjords.\nFlora and fauna.\nThe park protects wildlife including caribou, polar bears, peregrine falcon, and golden eagle.\nClimate.\nThe Arctic Cordillera represents one of Canada's most inhospitable climates, with temperatures in the -35 C range commonplace during dark, long Arctic winters.\nGet in.\nNunatsiavut is remote, accessible only by small aircraft or coastal ferries. The park, as the northernmost point in Labrador, is even further from the beaten path; access is by boat or chartered aircraft only.\nThe park borders Nunavik on Quebec's Ungava Peninsula - which is just as inaccessible. Nain, a tiny community in northern Nunatsiavut, Labrador is the usual jumping-off point to reach Torngat Park.\nFees and permits.\nVisitors must obtain permits, register and undergo an orientation before setting off on treks or boat trips.\nAs of 2024, fees have not been imposed for park admission.\nGet around.\nThere are no roads or infrastructure in the park. Access is primarily by boat or on foot; hikers are advised to travel with an Inuit bear guard.\nSpeedboats, longliners, polar bear guards, helicopter and fixed wing charters may be obtained through the base camp (\u260f, fax +1 709 896-5834).\nSee.\nThe Torngats are the highest mountains in Canada east of the Rockies. Mt Caubvick/D\u2019Iberville is the tallest at 1652 m (5437 feet); a few other peaks reach above 1538 m (5000 ft).\nThere are hundreds of archaeological sites in the park, some dating back almost 7,000 years. More recent remains include Moravian Mission sites, as well as Hudson Bay Company trading posts in Saglek and Nachvak fjords.\nAs the park is remote and remains in its natural state, it is well suited to wildlife watching - including birds, caribou and bears.\nDo.\nWilderness-oriented recreational activities include hiking, scrambling, kayaking and day or multi-day cultural and natural excursions, often with Inuit guides.\nHiking.\nThere are no roads, trails, or signs in Torngat Mountains National Park. There are, however, unmarked hiking routes and traditional Inuit travel routes marked by inuksuit (stone markers). Visitors can explore the park on guided or unguided day hikes and interpretive walks, overnight camping trips, multiday backpacking treks, and technical rock-climbing excursions.\nWith help and logistical support from Torngat Mountains Base Camp and Research Station, Parks Canada offers a range of day trips and overnight hiking opportunities in Torngat Mountains National Park.\nThere are many opportunities for backpacking in the vast and diverse terrain of Torngat Mountains National Park. Visitors can explore established hiking routes or map out their own route through different areas.\nParks Canada has prepared multi-day and shorter single-day hiking route descriptions for visitors. These outline known routes and offer some interpretation of the area. These route descriptions are reliable for describing the area that you are hiking in, but you must carry your own map, compass, and GPS. Do not use the route description as your only means of navigation.\nParks Canada also sets up satellite camps each summer to provide support for visitors that are looking for an overnight hiking experience. These satellite camps are big enough to hold 5-6 pup tents enclosed by a temporary bear fence. Visitors can do day hikes to and from these camps to areas of interest, ideally in the company of an Inuit bear guard.\nFor more information about hiking in the park, and the level of skill required for different routes, contact Torngat Mountains National Park or Torngats Base Camp and Research Station.\nBuy.\nThis is Arctic wilderness; there are no amenities within the park. The most basic provisions (groceries, equipment rental, accommodations, police and medical assistance) are available on a very limited basis in Nain and Kangiqsualujjuaq. If you need supplies, bring them with you.\nEat.\nBring what you need and leave no trace when you leave. Be bear aware: stored or cached foodstuffs may require bear-resistant containers.\nDrink.\nWhile there are many fresh water streams and ponds in the Torngat Mountains. this untested water should be boiled, treated (iodine or chlorine in warm water) or filtered (&lt;0.5 microns fine filter) before use.\nStay safe.\nThe mountains of northern Nunatsiavut are very much for the experienced, independent voyager. This is the Arctic, polar bear country where distances are vast (the park covers twice the area of Prince Edward Island, Canada's smallest province) and assistance is not always close at hand. Unless one is experienced with this region, it's best to rely on an Inuit guide when venturing far beyond the base camp. Native guides are permitted to carry firearms (where visitors are not) and know the area well.\nBring warm clothing and extra provisions, as adverse (unpredictable and bitterly cold) weather, sometimes even in summer, may substantially delay your transport out of the park. Under adverse conditions, outside assistance may be days away even in an emergency.\nCarry bear repellent and keep food out of reach of dangerous animals. Polar bears, black bears, foxes and wolves are all present in the area.\nLeave a copy of your plans with friends or family, so that they may contact police in an emergency (Nain's RCMP detachment is \u260f ; Parks Canada is \u260f in Nain and operates 24-hour emergency dispatch from faraway Jasper, Alberta at \u260f or \u260f).\nConnect.\nA satellite phone may be the only means of limited (and expensive) communication in this remote location. There are no satellite phones for rent or sale in Nain; the closest vendor is in Goose Bay."}
{"id": "153629", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153629", "title": "Ma'anshan", "text": "Ma'anshan (\u9a6c\u978d\u5c71, M\u01ce'\u0101nsh\u0101n) is a beautiful modern city in the downstream of the Yangtze River. It ranks among the wealthiest cities in Anhui Province. The city only gained town status in 1956, but is now home to over 2.2 million people. \nMa'anshan crosses the Yangtze River and borders Nanjing. It was once the prefecture of the Six Dynasties and the capital of the tenth dynasty. It has been known as the \"Jinling Barrier and the Key to Jiankang\" since ancient times. As early as the Six Dynasties, many famous officials, nobles, and sages stopped in Ma'anshan, leaving behind many historical sites and cultural relics.\nMa'anshan has successively won the title of National Civilized City, a core city in the Nanjing Metropolitan Area, a member city of the Yangtze River Delta City Cluster, a city along the Yangtze River Economic Belt, a gateway city for the industrial transfer demonstration zone in the Wanjiang City Belt, a national pilot city for the standardization of public cultural services, and a national science and technology It has been honored as a pilot city for city development, an international tourism and culture demonstration zone in southern Anhui, and one of the first batch of national information consumption demonstration cities.\nMa'anshan is one of the top ten steel bases in China, where Maanshan Iron and Steel Co., Ltd. is located. Ma'anshan Port is one of the top ten ports on the Yangtze River and the first port on the Wanjiang River. It is an important steel circulation base in China. Ma'anshan Zhengpu Port follows the two deep-water ports of Shanghai and Nanjing. Later, it was the last 10,000-ton deepwater shoreline terminal on the Yangtze River.\nUnderstand.\nIt was once famous for Ma Steel, and is now developing into a port city. The economy is relies half on industry and half on agriculture. The farmlands surrounding the city offer beautiful scenery. The city is adjacent to Nanjing in the east, Wuhu in the west, Hefei and Chuzhou in the north, and Xuancheng in the south, with a total area of 4,049 square kilometers. Osmanthus is the city flower of Maanshan City and is widely planted in Maanshan. With more rainfall and a mild climate, Maanshan is suitable for the growth of osmanthus, which is usually in full bloom from mid-September to National Day. Balsam camphor is the city tree of Maanshan. The Balsam camphor tree is loved by Maanshan residents, who often regard it as a landscape tree, or geomancy tree, which means protection from evil, longevity, and good luck.\nHistory.\nDuring the Western Zhou Dynasty, Ma'anshan belonged to the state of Wu. The Yangtze River culture has a long history, and about 300,000 years ago, the Hexian ape-man was here to reproduce. Successive generations of writers and artists have lingered in the land of Maanshan, and left behind many masterpieces of the world.\nMa'anshan City has a long history, and the city's name comes from history. According to legend, during the Chu-Han War, the Chu Overlord Xiang Yu was trapped in Gaixia and was surrounded by enemies. He retreated to the Wujiang River in He County. He asked a fisherman to ferry his beloved horse Wuzui Horse to the other side. Later, he felt shameless. Seeing Jiangdong's elders, he committed suicide and died. The black horse missed its master, rolled over and killed itself. The saddle fell to the ground and turned into a mountain. Ma'anshan got its name from this. Among the existing local historical materials in Ma'anshan City, the earliest record of the name of Ma'anshan is in the Ming Dynasty's \"Taiping Fuzhi\u00b7Yu Di Zhi\": Ma'anshan is at the port of Cihu in the northwest of Fucheng. The old shrine was built in the second year of Chiwu.\nAs of the end of 2022, Maanshan City had a resident population of 2.186 million. It is a city of scattered ethnic minorities, with 43 ethnic minorities in the city's resident population, including about 13,000 Hui, accounting for 65 per cent of the total ethnic minority population.\nIt has a subtropical humid monsoon climate with four distinct seasons, warm and humid.\nGet around.\nTake high-speed railway - Nanjing can be arrived in about 15 minutes, Shanghai and Hangzhou in 1.5 hours and Beijing in about 4 hours.\nDo.\nXiangquan Hot Spring Resort.\nXiangquan Hot Spring Resort is located in Xiangquan Town, He County, Ma'anshan City, Anhui Province. It is \"an ancient hot spring with graceful egrets inhabiting it.\" The hot spring has a long history. It is located in the egret habitat area. It combines precious resources with the ecological environment. Good place\". Xiangquan Hot Spring is a four-star hot spring tourist resort integrating hot spring health care, catering and accommodation, business conferences, health care, leisure and entertainment.\nXiangquan Hot Spring enjoys the reputation of \"the most fragrant spring\". Xiangquan Hot Spring is named after the aroma of chicken. It is a radon-containing calcium sulfate type hot spring. The water quality is clear and crystal clear, and the water temperature is 47\u00b0C-50\u00b0C all year round.\nTickets: start at 120\nOpen: 9:00-23:00\nContact number: 0555-5523777\nDaqingshan Wildlife World.\nDaqingshan Wildlife World is located in Daqingshan Scenic Area, Dangtu County, Ma'anshan City, the second hometown of Shixian Taibai. It is designed and built using the forest landforms of Daqingshan and introducing international advanced concepts. The park covers an area of more than 1,020 square meters and introduces There are more than 200 species and more than 3,000 animals from all over the world. It will become a comprehensive tourism project integrating wildlife viewing, science education, leisure and entertainment, human-animal interaction, cultural tourism and sports.\nTickets: starting from 88\nOpen: 9:00-18:00\nContact number: 0555-6588888\nZhengpugang Shengxiang Ice and Snow World.\nSnow Cube Ice and Snow World is a sports and leisure themed venue that mainly builds a large-scale indoor ski resort and is equipped with entertainment, conferences, expansion training, ski sports schools and other facilities.\nIn addition to beginner ski trails, intermediate and advanced leisure ski trails and teaching ski trails, the ski resort also has specially built snow play facilities such as the Dream Ice and Snow Fairy City for children, ice slides, ice and snow mazes, and ice and snow-shaped buildings. It feels like you are in \"Ice and Snow\" The world of \"Romance\" satisfies children's whims about the world of ice and snow.\nIn addition, TusQiaobo Ice and Snow World in Zhengpugang New District also has a ski school, which draws on advanced foreign skiing systems, theories and methods, and hires outstanding ski instructors certified by relevant national institutions, allowing citizens to master skiing skills in the shortest time. The endless fun of leading the way on skis. In the ski hall, there are also supporting facilities such as an ice and snow food court, a VR game experience area, an ice and snow exhibition hall, etc., so that every visitor to the \"Ice and Snow World\" can enjoy all the ice and snow in a \"one-stop\" way.\nThe ski resort has introduced the internationally advanced \"Magic Carpet\" towing capacity equipment, and adopted internationally advanced snow-making and refrigeration equipment and technology. The venue is maintained at about minus 2 degrees Celsius throughout the year. The nearly half-meter-thick snow track is open all year round, and the leisurely falling snowflakes and The landscape of the ski pavilion merges into a spectacular \"forest and snowfield\", allowing you to enjoy the excitement of skiing and the fun of playing in the snow even in the scorching sun.\nOpen from Monday to Friday 09:00-19:00 (no entry at 16:00)\nOpen from Saturday to Sunday 09:00-21:00 (no entry at 18:00)\nContact number:0555-8880699\nThe Yangtze River Evernight City.\nThe Yangtze River Evernight City project integrates the development vitality and cultural characteristics of the Yangtze River Delta, and combines the unique landscape, humanistic history, folk customs and intangible cultural heritage elements of the Ma'anshan Cai Shi Alum Ecological Tourism Zone in a \"point-line-surface\" distribution in the neighborhood, through scene art Performing arts are revitalized and created. The Yangtze Evernight City stands on the bank of the Yangtze River in Ma'anshan, Anhui Province. The scenic area covers an area of 134 acres, with a total area of 89,332m and a total block length of 1,100 meters. It integrates exquisite national trend scenes, light art interaction, performance art interpretation, technology experience, special food, and Internet celebrities. It is a comprehensive leisure tourism resort that integrates check-in, water views, naked-eye 3D holography, and immersive parade performances.\nThe Yangtze Evernight City project uses a new development of thinking and models to present the urban culture, natural scenery, and historical heritage of the 11 provinces in the Yangtze River Basin in a three-dimensional way. Through the overall layout of one street, one volume, one paradise and four squares, it provides a scene-based immersive experience. Create an innovative scene space model to create a scene and entertainment every step of the way. Fully activate the night-time economy. Let Ma'anshan, the \"city that never sleeps on the Yangtze River,\" the pearl, bloom and dazzle.\nfree\nOpen Monday to Friday 17:00-22:00\nOpen Saturday-Sunday 11:00-22:00\nLanhai Bathing and Steaming Hot Spring.\nThis is a leisure place with Internet cafes, leisure halls, foot massages, luxury cinemas, swimming pools, children's playgrounds, billiards, and book bars.\nOpen: all day\nContact number:0555-6068888\nMa'anshan Municipal Museum.\nMa'anshan Municipal Museum is located in the cultural center of the beautiful Municipal Park, integrated with the library and grand theater. Its exquisite architectural design blends with the ecological environment and is shaded by green shadows. The museum was established in 1989. It is a local comprehensive museum integrating collection, research and exhibition. The new museum started construction in 2006 and opened for operation on October 1, 2008. The building area is nearly 11,000 square meters, including 3,000 square meters of exhibition area, 400 square meters of film and television hall, and 3,000 square meters of leisure auxiliary building. The collection contains nearly 10,000 pieces (sets) of various cultural relics. In September 2018, the China Museum Association decided to approve Ma'anshan Museum as a national second-level museum.\nApril 1st - December 31st\nOpen: Tuesday-Friday, Sunday 09:00-17:00\nOpen: Saturday 09:00-20:00\nannual\nClosed: on Monday\nContact number:0555-8355979\nBuy.\nMa'anshan has many commercial districts with local characteristics, such as old streets with historical and cultural atmosphere, modern shopping malls and traditional markets, making the shopping experience more diverse. Because of its geographical location and cultural background, it has unique local products, such as handicrafts with regional characteristics, local agricultural and sideline products, etc. These products usually reflect the local traditional culture and customs. Ma'anshan has a number of modern shopping malls and department stores, offering international and local brands of goods, including clothing, home furnishings, electronic products and other products to suit the needs of different consumers. Shopping areas usually have a unique atmosphere where you can experience local lifestyle and cultural characteristics. Some shopping areas also regularly hold events, performances or festivals to add to the fun and entertainment of shopping. Compared with some big cities, Ma'anshan's commodity prices may be more affordable, attracting many tourists from surrounding areas to come shopping.\nSesame oil is a specialty industry in Hanshan County. Since the 1960s, a busy scene has appeared in Qimen Village, Huanfeng Town, where \"tens of miles of sesame oil is fragrant and thousands of people are busy selling oil.\" Sesame oil has also made Qimen a city with a thousand-year history The historic little market town is famous everywhere.\nIn the Tongzha and Baoshan areas of the county, folk paper-cutting craftsmanship is more common. Use a piece of tissue paper and a pair of scissors to skillfully cut out various flowers, birds, animals, fish and shrimps, as well as costume characters, fat dolls and other graphic decorative arts to beautify life and are deeply loved by the working people.\nEat.\nMa'anshan, a city located in Anhui Province, China, has a rich history of food culture. Ma'anshan has an advantageous geographical location, located on the lower reaches of the Yangtze River, with a suitable climate and rich products, providing unique conditions for the formation of gourmet culture.\nThe history of Ma'anshan cuisine can be traced back to ancient times. During the Spring and Autumn Period and the Warring States Period, this place belonged to the junction of Wu and Chu. Its unique geographical location made it the intersection of various cultures. For thousands of years, Ma'anshan cuisine has absorbed the characteristics of Anhui cuisine, Jiangsu cuisine, Sichuan cuisine and other major cuisines, forming a unique taste.\nMa'anshan cuisine is characterized by unique cooking techniques and local materials, focusing on the perfect combination of color, aroma, taste, shape and utensils. Among them, fish heads from Xuejiawa, dried tea from Caishiji, egg pancakes from the train station, wild duck vegetable rice from Zhujiajiao, etc. are all representatives of Ma'anshan delicacies.\nRestaurant areas.\nGu Street, located in Hong Kong City, is a household name in Ma On Shan. It can be regarded as the \"master of food streets\". Hidden in this street are many delicious restaurants that feed the souls of Ma On Shan people. Any one of them can satisfy your heart and stomach.\nCaishiji dried tea.\nOrigin and history: Caishiji dried tea originated during the Jiaqing period of the Qing Dynasty. It was one of the tributes made by the Qing Dynasty and has a history of more than 200 years. Materials and production: Caishiji dried tea is mainly made of dried beans, with chicken juice, star anise, cinnamon, licorice, fennel, osmanthus, sugar, monosodium glutamate, potassium sorbate and other seasonings. The soybean slurry is first filtered and then used It is pressed by traditional methods and refined by modern craftsmanship. Characteristics and taste: Caishiji dried tea is red in color, hard and delicate in texture, full of toughness in the mouth, fragrant and mellow in the mouth, and becomes more flavorful the more you chew it. It is a snack with local characteristics and unique flavor of soy products.\nDrink.\nMa'anshan is located in the central part of Anhui Province and is one of the famous liquor-producing areas in the country. Ma'anshan liquor has a long history, with a brewing tradition as early as the Han Dynasty. There are two main types of pit mud: Jinshan pit mud and rock tea pit mud. The liquor produced in Jinshan Jiao Ni usually has a mellow taste and becomes even more perfect after aging; while the Yancha Jiao Ni is famous for its rich aroma, which is endlessly memorable.\nBahan Jiuwu Zhi Baijiu, as a Yanghe brand liquor, originates from Jiangsu. This production area has a long brewing tradition and rich brewing resources. Jiangsu Green Spring Winery is the winery that produces this wine, and is famous for its exquisite brewing technology and excellent quality. Liquor is not only a beverage, but also a representative of culture. Brand and origin are one of the factors that users pay great attention to when choosing drinks.\nBahan Jiuwu Zhi Baijiu is loved by people for its rich aroma and soft taste. Every sip of wine seems to lead people to a fragrant resort, the aroma is permeating and intoxicating. The richness of the wine gives it a calm texture, making you want to drink it all in one gulp. Whether it is tasting or tasting, Bahan Jiuwu Zhi Baijiu can bring users an unprecedented taste experience.\nThe price of Bahan Jiuwu Zhi Baijiu is very affordable, only 59.00 yuan per bottle. It is a very attractive choice for many people who like to taste wine. This white wine is suitable for every occasion, whether it is paired with Chinese food, given as a gift, or enjoyed on festive occasions, it can display its unique flavor and charm. It can not only meet users' needs for high-quality liquor, but also satisfy users' pursuit of affordable prices.\nPlaces to drink.\nAddress:No. 1-16, Peony Garden, Chongyang Road, Huashan District, Ma'anshan City\nOpen:19:00-02:30\nAddress:Building 53, Maker Cultural Park, No. 1599 Hudong Road, Huashan District, Ma'anshan City, Anhui Province\nOpen:20:00-04:00\nAddress:Room 109, Building 81098, Huashan District Pedestrian Street, Ma'anshan City, Anhui Province\nOpen:18:00-02:30\nAddress:No. 1, Building 14-1, Pingshan New Village, Caimu Road\nOpen:18:30-01:00\nAddress\uff1aRoom 407, 4th Floor, Building 3, Dahua International Plaza, No. 1599 Hubei East Road, Huashan District, Ma'anshan City, Anhui Province\nOpen:summer:18:30-02:30\nWinter:18:00-02:30\nSleep.\nFinding tranquility amidst the bustle has become a continuation of the travel experience for business guests, protecting a peaceful home for you who are rushing around in the busy city.\nThere are many clear bars and high-end restaurants in the hotel, and the surrounding areas are crowded with people to eat, drink and have fun. The hotel rooms are exquisite and equipped with self-service laundry rooms, bars, fancy coffees, etc. In the hotel, experience the infinite possibilities of life and ignite the unique splendor of life. Integrating localized features into the design and experience naturally combines business travel and travel attributes. You can feel the charm of this city from the well-designed lobby, the details of the corridors, and the scenery outside the room window. While satisfying the quality accommodation experience, it allows you to create your own unique city memory in every small detail. Add a touch of color to your journey with each city's characteristic architecture and local cultural experiences. Let you always look forward to the next city.\nModern equipment such as the human body sensing system, intelligent voice guest control, and the \"Huazhanggui\" self-service all-in-one machine with \"30-second check-in, 0-second check-out\" also add a sense of technology and luxury to the residence. The hotel restaurant buffet features both Chinese and Western food, especially the customized Ma'anshan specialties, bringing guests a personalized service experience of \"one city, one taste\".\nEach functional area is independent but not closed, which not only increases the comfort but also improves the quality of the space. All guest rooms are in relaxed and bright colors. The hotel also provides guests with free parking, exquisite breakfast, bar, various handmade coffees, laundry rooms, conference rooms, etc. After work, you can rest and enjoy tea in the leisure area, which will bring you physical and mental benefits. Vacation-like enjoyment, everything is just for guests to achieve a perfect and fashionable journey.\nPlaces to sleep.\nAddress:No. 606, Hunan Road, Yushan District, Ma'anshan City, Anhui Province \nAddress:Building 8, No. 25 Hunan Road, Yushan District, Ma'anshan City, Anhui Province \nAddress:No. 555-2, Hongqi Middle Road, Yushan District, Ma'anshan City, Anhui Province\nAddress:No. 300, Yushan East Road, Huashan District, Ma'anshan City, Anhui Province\nAddress:No. 88, Wudanggang Road, Huashan District, Ma'anshan City, Anhui Province\nAddress:No. 589 Park Road, Huashan District, Ma'anshan City, Anhui Province\nConnect.\nPhone.\nChina's country code is 86. Ma'anshan's area code is 0555. \nGo next.\nChina's high-speed railroad is developing very well. It's easy to travel to nearby cities by high-speed rail. For example, the same provincial capital city Hefei. It takes 2 and a half hours by high-speed train and more than one hundred RMB."}
{"id": "153631", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153631", "title": "M\u0103cin Mountains National Park", "text": "M\u0103cin Mountains National Park is a national park in Northern Dobruja, Romania.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nThe national park was set up in 2003.\nLandscape.\nThe M\u0103cin mountains are geologically the oldest in Romania. They feature a wide variety of landscapes including arid rocky areas, canyons, and weathered granite.\nFlora and fauna.\nThis national park has features of Balkan, Mediterranean and Central European forest landscapes, including forest-steppe woods with Dobrogean peony, and a variety of plants including downy oak, flowering ash, sessile oak, Dobrogean olive, spirea, nettle tree, viburnum, and Chequer. Its Dobrogean Beech forest is the only one in the world. \nClimate.\nThe M\u0103cin Mountains' climate is continentally-tempered with steppe climate influences in the south of the park and Mediterranean climate influences on the peaks and mountain ridge.\nFees and permits.\nTourists must pay 10 RON Per person (available for two days)\nLocals can enter in the park for free.\nGet around.\nM\u0103cin Mountains National Park is crossed by many mountain trails arranged (with campsites) in order to facilitate the access of tourists safely to the chosen destination.\nTourist Trails\nThematic Trails\nOther Trails\nSee.\nM\u0103cin Mountains National Park has a variety of tourist attractions including:\nDo.\nM\u0103cin Mountains has a variety of activities including rock climbing, hiking and mountain biking.\nRock Climbing Areas and Trails"}
{"id": "153634", "revid": "35982", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153634", "title": "Harry Potter tourism", "text": "Harry Potter is a series of fantasy novels, written by J. K. Rowling, and made into a series of eight films from 2001 to 2011.\nUnderstand.\nThe \"Harry Potter\" stories are set in a fictionalised version of the United Kingdom in the late 20th century, where some locations are inaccessible or invisible to muggles (people without magical abilities). Most of the towns and villages featured in the series, including muggle settlements, were created by Rowling, but are generally either based on real places or are archetypical of a particular region.\nThe first book and film in the series were titled \"Harry Potter and the Philospher's Stone\" in the original British releases, in reference to the legends of alchemy in European folklore, where the philospher's stone was a substance that enabled its bearer to transmute base metals into gold, and create an elixir of life that could confer immortality. However, the title was changed to \"Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone\" for the American releases, a decision made by the American publisher of the books out of fear that American children would associate the term \"philosopher\" with a person who is boring and nerdy and be put off from reading the book. For this article, the original British title and terms will be used.\nIt is possible to visit many of the places that inspired the books. The series was filmed in a variety of locations around the UK and Ireland at the behest of J.K. Rowling.\nStarting from 2016, a new prequel film series entitled \"Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them\" is being made, with Rowling making her screenwriting debut for the series. The first film in the series went on to win an Academy Award for Best Costume Design, achieving what had eluded all eight films in the original series (which received a total of 12 Oscar nominations, but never won). While originally intended to span five films, the third film in the prequel series \"Fantastic Beasts: Secrets of Dumbledore\" (2022) was a box office bomb, and production of the last two films has been cancelled.\n \"For more information, see the \"Get in\" section in the United Kingdom article.\"\nGet in.\nUnited Kingdom.\nAt J. K. Rowling's insistence, the original eight films were produced almost entirely in the United Kingdom. \nBy plane.\nMany buses, trains, coaches and car rental facilities make it a convenient starting place for exploring Britain, particularly for visiting attractions in the London area such as Warner Bros. Studio Tour and King's Cross station. In addition, Oxford is only about 50 miles (80 km) west of Heathrow.\nMost Scottish locations are in northern Scotland with Glasgow being the nearest major airport with car rental facilities.\nBy train.\nLondon St Pancras International has the Eurostar from Paris Gare du Nord (North Station) in France.\nLondon Victoria is on the London Underground Victoria \u00a0VIC\u00a0 line to King's Cross St. Pancras (for both train stations). About 100 yards (90 metres) from the station is the Victoria Coach Station which offers a coach to just about every city in Britain.\nLondon King's Cross has connections from Heathrow Airport via the London Underground Piccadilly \u00a0PIC\u00a0 line. There is also a direct 3-hour train to Durham.\nWatford Junction is on the London Overground Lioness line (formerly Watford DC) to Willesden Junction and London Euston.\nOxford has connections from London Marylebone.\n\"See the Cliffs of Moher listing below.\"\nRepublic of Ireland.\nAs of 2023, the only location in the Republic of Ireland that was used for the \"Harry Potter\" films was the Cliffs of Moher on the west coast between Galway and Limerick.\nThemed attractions.\nAll these themed locations are home to souvenir shops where you can purchase \"Harry Potter\" memorabilia. All of these also sell a real-world version of butterbeer, a drink that is featured in the books and movies. Unlike the book version, real butterbeer is not alcoholic.\nWarner Bros. Studio Tour.\nLondon.\nPrebooking tickets on the Warner Bros. Studio Tour website is vital as this is a very popular attraction.\nThe Warner Bros. Studio Tour can be easily reached from the \u00a0LIO\u00a0 and \u00a0CIR\u00a0\u00a0DIS\u00a0\u00a0VIC\u00a0 train stations with regular shuttle coaches. During the ride, there is an introductory video from Tom Felton who portrayed Draco Malfoy in the films.\nYou see the different sets, costumes and props at your own pace. You can grab a passport and find each of the embossed stamps and all of the hidden golden snitches.\nOn-site catering is overpriced, so it's a good idea to have lunch before visiting. Do try a sweet non-alcoholic butterbeer before visiting the external sets including the Dursleys' house at 4 Privet Drive and the Knight Bus.\nThe Wizarding World of Harry Potter at Universal Studios.\nThemed areas at Universal Studios parks:\n\"For the Edinburgh sites, see Edinburgh inspiration.\"\nFilm locations.\nScotland.\nAll of these sites are in northern Scottish Highlands with the nearest major city being Glasgow. It is recommended to fly into Glasgow Airport and hire a car to reach these.\nEngland.\nLondon.\nBoth train stations are in the north London borough of Camden and are well-connected by the London Underground and buses. On the London Underground, the station is jointly referred to as King's Cross St. Pancras \u00a0CIR\u00a0\u00a0MET\u00a0\u00a0NOR\u00a0\u00a0PIC\u00a0\u00a0VIC\u00a0.\nLondon Euston \u00a0NOR\u00a0\u00a0VIC\u00a0\u00a0LIO\u00a0 is also nearby and on the Northern and Victoria lines. Nearby Euston Square \u00a0CIR\u00a0\u00a0MET\u00a0 which is within walking distance of London Euston has more connections.\nTake a Northern line train on the London Underground to London Waterloo \u00a0BAK\u00a0\u00a0JUB\u00a0\u00a0NOR\u00a0 to see the Millennium Bridge and then go further south towards Guildford and Woking in Surrey to see Surbiton.\nOxford.\nOxford is a popular destination for students and tourists alike. As such it's well-connected by public transport, especially from London. With a small city centre, most attractions (both \"Harry Potter\"-related and not) are within walking distance. There are also a number of Harry Potter walking tours and merchandise shops on Broad St.\nBy road: On the M40 motorway from London and Birmingham, as well as the A40 from Cheltenham (and the Cotswolds). Oxford city centre is notoriously bad for driving with its one-way system and parking is both expensive and difficult in the city centre, so it's recommended to use one of the 5 park &amp; rides and take a bus instead.\nBy train: You can reach in the city centre via London Paddington. Or via London Marylebone and a bus from the adjacent Water Eaton Park &amp; Ride.\nBy coach: There is the Oxford Tube from London Victoria to in the centre of Oxford. Alternatively, The Airline coach goes directly from both London Heathrow and London Gatwick to Oxford.\nWest Country.\nJ. K. Rowling grew up in the West Country and her work is heavily influenced by the region. The Dursley family, Harry's Muggle relatives, are named after the town of Dursley in Gloucestershire. Rubeus Hagrid's distinctive West Country accent was inspired by a biker Rowling met in a pub and John Nettleship, her chemistry teacher at Wyedean School was the main inspiration for Professor Snape.\nGloucester is the county town of Gloucestershire and the cathedral is within walking distance of the city centre.\nBy road: Gloucester is on the M5 motorway from Birmingham to Exeter. The services are a great place to stop along the way. The A40 goes from London to Fishguard in Wales via Oxford and Gloucester.\nBy train: Gloucester train station can be reached directly from London Paddington or Bristol Temple Meads.\nBy bus: Gloucester Transport Hub has many bus and coach services from London Victoria and elsewhere.\nCoppett Hill is near Goodrich in South Herefordshire. It is a short 5 mi drive from Ross-on-Wye and a longer 22 mi drive from Gloucester.\nBy road: On the A40 from Gloucester and the B4234 from Ross-on-Wye.\nSleep: Ross-on-Wye has a few B&amp;Bs and pubs, but there is more accommodation in Gloucester or Hereford.\nChippenham can also be reached by train from London Paddington or Bristol Temple Meads. Or you can take junction 17 from the M40 motorway.\nBy road: Chippenham and Lacock are on the A350 and about 60 mi from Bournemouth.\nBy coach: National Express coach services to Chippenham: Northampton to Bristol (302), Bath to London (403) and Penzance to London (404).\nBy bus: The X34 bus goes from Chippenham to Frome via Lacock.\nNorth Yorkshire.\nGoathland is a small village inside the North York Moors national park that is about 9 mi from Whitby and 14 mi from Pickering.\nBy road: From Whitby or Pickering via the A169 and Cow Wath Bank.\nBy bus: 840 Coastliner bus from Whitby.\nBy train: Northern Rail has trains from Newcastle-upon-Tyne and Middlesbrough to Whitby. For direct trains to Goathland, the North Yorkshire Moors Railway runs summer steam trains from Pickering via Levisham, Newtondale, Goathland and Grosmont. Most terminate at Grosmont and a couple per day continue into Whitby.\nEat/Drink: Goathland has a pub and a tea room.\nSleep: Goathland has a few small hotels and B&amp;Bs for overnight staying.\nMalham is a very rural small village about 11 mi from Skipton and 38 mi from Leeds.\nBy road: You can drive the rural A65 from Skipton.\nBy bus: 210 bus takes about 35 minutes from Skipton.\nBy train: Northern Rail has a train from Leeds to Skipton and from there you can take the 210 bus.\nBy foot: Malham is on the famous Pennine Way walking route.\nNorthern England.\nAlnwick (pronounced \"Annick\") is a town about 30 mi away from the Scottish border at Berwick-upon-Tweed and about 35 mi from Newcastle Upon Tyne.\nBy car: On the A1 road from Newcastle Upon Tyne.\nBy train: Take a train down to Alnmouth from Edinburgh Waverley station or up from Newcastle-upon-Tyne station and then a bus to visit.\nBy bus: Arriva Bus X15 MAX runs hourly from Newcastle upon Tyne Haymarket, taking 90 min via Gosforth and Morpeth, as of July 2022. Alternate buses continue north up the A1 to Beal (for Lindisfarne), Scremerston and Berwick-upon-Tweed.\nSleep: There is upmarket accommodation in Alnwick:\nDurham is a cathedral city with many direct connections. The cathedral itself is a short walk from the train station on the meander of the River Wear.\nBy train: Durham can be reached directly by a 3-hour train from London King's Cross. There is also a 1-hour and 15-minute direct train from Leeds if you are also visiting Malham.\nBy coach: There is also a National Express bus from London Victoria if you need to pay less and are willing to travel a full 7 hours straight. You could watch 2 of the \"Harry Potter\" films back-to-back in that time.\nEast Midlands.\nChesterfield is a large town in the East Midlands. Hardwick Hall is a National Trust property between Chesterfield and Mansfield and requires a car to get there. \nIf you planning on also seeing Lacock Abbey and other National Trust properties, it may be cheaper to purchase the National Trust membership for a month.\nWales.\nFreshwater West is rural and the best way to reach there seems to be by car. There are two car parks though they can be very busy during surfing season.\nIreland.\nThe Cliffs of Moher are in a rural spot overlooking the Atlantic Ocean on the west coast about 7 km south of Doolin between Galway and Limerick.\nBy road: You can drive the N67 road to the cliffs or take the 350 bus which runs 6 times a day from Galway.\nBy plane: Galway itself no longer has an airport, but there are frequent trains and buses from Dublin.\nBy coach: Alternatively, there are coach tours which take you directly to the Cliffs of Moher and back again.\nEat and drink: Doolin has a few Irish pubs which offer food, drink and live music entertainment. Some establishments may be cash-only, so be sure to have a few euros on hand.\nEdinburgh inspiration.\nJ.K. Rowling lived in Edinburgh while writing many of the books, and several locations in the city may have provided inspiration, especially around the historic Old Town.\nAll of these inspirational places are within walking distance of Edinburgh Waverley train station.\nTheatre.\n\"Harry Potter and the Cursed Child\" is a two-part play performed at the Palace Theatre in London and the Lyric Theatre in New York. There are also performances in Hamburg and Tokyo.\nThe play is a direct sequel to \"Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows\". Now as an adult and a father, Harry Potter grapples with the challenges of parenthood and the legacy of his past.\nThe play is split into 2 parts which can be watched back-to-back or booked over separate days. Each part has a 20-minute intermission:\nThe first part: 2 hours and 40 minutes\nThe second part: 2 hours and 35 minutes\nThere is exclusive merchandise for sale, you can also buy a copy of the playscript from any major bookstore or as an e-book.\nBuy.\nThe Warner Bros. Studio Tour and Universal Studios attractions have gift shops. There are also these two stores in London:\nLearn.\nParseltongue, the language spoken by the various characters when talking to snakes, is, perhaps surprisingly, not just gibberish but an actual language that was constructed by University of Cambridge linguistics professor Francis Nolan for the films. Given the limited amount of Parseltongue dialogue in the film, the vocabulary is, of course limited, but there are nevertheless several Harry Potter fan groups who have dedicated significant resources towards learning and studying the language, and several online translators to Parseltongue are available. As snakes do not know how to write, there is, of course, no written language for Parseltongue.\nSee also.\nJ. K. Rowling maintains the Pottermore website dedicated to the \"Harry Potter\" universe with several fun activities to keep fans occupied."}
{"id": "153649", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153649", "title": "Jianshui", "text": "Jianshui County (\u5efa\u6c34\u53bf \"Jianshui xian\") is a division of Honghe Prefecture in Southeastern Yunnan. Numerous temples, traditional residences, bridges and other historic sites survive in and around the old town, making Jianshui a useful stop on an overland journey between Kunming and Hekou, on the border with Vietnam.\nUnderstand.\nAlthough the county seat has its own name (it's called Lin'an Town \u4e34\u5b89\u9547 \"Lin'an zhen\"), the name Jianshui is commonly used to refer to Lin'an as well, as it is generally the case with Chinese county seats.\nLin'an Town lies in the fertile valley of the Lin'an River in southern Yunnan. The river crosses Jianshui County from west to east, while much of the surrounding county is higher and more mountainous. To the south, the terrain drops toward the Red River Valley.\nSince the Yuan Dynasty, Lin'an has developed into an important regional centre in southern Yunnan, with a strong tradition of Confucian education that is still reflected in sites such as the Confucian Temple and the former imperial examination hall.\nAlthough most of the old city wall was demolished during the 20th century, the traditional locations of Lin'an's four gates remain important landmarks in the old town. Chaoyang Gate Tower, the eastern gate tower, is the best-preserved historic structure of the four. \nClimate.\nJianshui has a mild subtropical monsoon climate typical of southern Yunnan. Temperatures are generally moderate throughout the year, with relatively mild winters and warm summers. Average annual temperatures are around 16\u201321 \u00b0C. Most rainfall falls between May and October, with annual precipitation generally around 800\u20131,200 mm. The drier months from November to April are generally sunnier and are often more convenient for sightseeing.\nGet in.\nJianshui is served by the standard-gauge railway linking Kunming with Mengzi and Hekou on the Vietnamese border. Jianshui Railway Station is the main station for regular passenger services. Train schedules and fares change, so check the official China Railway 12306 service for current information.\nThe historic metre-gauge railway uses Lin'an Station near the old town. It is primarily used by the Jianshui tourist train rather than regular passenger rail services. Trains run from Lin'an Station via Shuanglong Bridge and Xianghuiqiao to Tuanshan; normally two round trips operate each day, with three during peak tourist periods. Current schedules should be checked before travel.\nGet around.\nCentral Jianshui is fairly compact, and most sights in the old town are within walking distance of one another. Local buses connect the old town with newer parts of the city and major transport hubs. Some buses accept WeChat and Alipay QR-code payments, and electronic bus-stop displays are available on parts of the network. Routes and schedules can change, so check current information locally.\nTaxis and ride-hailing services are also available and are useful for destinations outside the historic centre. Shared electric bikes are available in some outlying tourist areas, including around Wulong Lake and the Shuanglong Bridge rice-field park, but they are not provided inside Jianshui Old Town.\nFor sights farther from the centre, such as Tuanshan Village, Shuanglong Bridge and Swallow Cave, allow additional travel time. The Jianshui tourist train is a sightseeing service rather than regular public transport and connects Lin'an Station with several attractions west of the old town.\nSee.\nThe Four Gates of Lin'an.\nAlthough Lin'an's city wall no longer survives, the locations of its four historic gates remain important landmarks in the old city's street network. Of the original gate towers, Chaoyang Gate in the east is the best-preserved historic structure.\nBuy.\nThis town specializes in pottery. It is called \"Zi Tao Pottery\" or purple pottery. The pottery is nice and of very good quality. Make sure you get a handmade one. Get it from one of the masters in town or at a cheaper rate from some of the shops around.\nEat.\nAt the old train station, there is an excellent store selling crossing-the-bridge-noodles. Be sure to check them out."}
