{"id": "153798", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153798", "title": "Hahei", "text": "Hahei is a seaside town in Mercury Bay on the east side of the Coromandel Peninsula in northern New Zealand. Cruising boats depart for trips to Te Whanganui-A-Hei Marine Reserve and its abundant sea-life. Hahei is a popular holiday destination, with a white sandy beach and sheltered aspect, resulting in safe sheltered swimming waters. The famous Hot Water Beach, where geothermal springs come up through the sand around low tide, is 6 km away.\nUnderstand.\nCoromandel Peninsula is on the north-eastern coast of the North Island, due east from Auckland. Hahei's resident population is around 300, but in peak holiday periods the population swells nine times. The town centre has a gift shop, a general store with a petrol station and a takeaways bar, and a dive shop. Holiday-related businesses include kayaking, snorkelling and boat trips to Cathedral Cove and the Marine Reserve.\nGet in.\nTurn off the main road between Tairua and Whitianga at Whenuakite. About half way from there to Hahei, a road turns off to Hot Water Beach. Hahei is a 40-minute drive from Whitianga, and a 15-minute drive from Ferry Landing, which is a short passenger ferry trip from Whitianga.\nBy bus.\nGo Kiwi runs a once per day shuttle from Auckland via Auckland Airport.\nSee.\nAs of October 2023 this track was closed and potentially not re-opening due to heavy rain causing land slip and the track therefore not being safe. \nThe only access to Cathedral Cove now is by boat.\nSleep.\nThere are numerous accommodation providers not listed here."}
{"id": "153802", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153802", "title": "Bewdley", "text": "Bewdley is a market town in the Wyre Forest District in Worcestershire, on the banks of the River Severn near Kidderminster. Bewdley is a popular tourist destination and is known for the Bewdley Bridge, designed by Thomas Telford, and the well preserved Georgian riverside.\nUnderstand.\nBewdley lies on the River Severn with the Bewdley Bridge connecting the east and west sides.\nGet in.\nBewdley is 3 miles (4.8 km) west of Kidderminster, 10 miles (16 km) north of Worcester and 25 miles (40 km) southwest of Birmingham. \nDrink.\nBewdley is also home to its own beer brewery which offers a small tour and opportunities to ask questions."}
{"id": "153808", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153808", "title": "Great Escarpment (Angola)", "text": "The Angolan section of Southern Africa's Great Escarpment consists of two primary mountain ranges, the Serra da Leba and Serra de Chela. \nUnderstand.\nThe Great Escarpment's northernmost reaches extend into Angola, but it is an enormous geographic feature throughout the whole of Southern Africa. It ranges southwards through Namibia, cuts through South Africa, and then extends north along the border of Mozambique and Zimbabwe. It surrounds the Central Southern African Plateau (sitting at over 2,000 m above sea level), with steep mountains descending towards Angola's coastal regions.\nHistory.\nThe Escarpment is old, having formed along with the formation of the Atlantic Ocean some 160 million years ago. The steep mountains you see today have been shaped by the gradual rising of the plateau over the past 20 million years, and the very slow accompanying process of erosion from coastal winds.\nLandscape.\nThe Escarpment is the zone right between Angola's central plateau (which includes Lubango), home to the country's savanna landscapes, and the steep mountains and hills descending towards the flat coastal regions. The immediate mountainous drop from the plateau is steepest in this region, with the Serra da Chela mountains being the country's steepest and most dramatic. The highest point in Angola, however, is Mount Moco, in the Huambo Province.\nFlora and fauna.\nThe mountain regions are rocky, with patches of dry woodlands. The low forest was once home to elephants, lions, and cheetahs, but as with most of the Angolan wild, these were hunted and poached into oblivion during the 30 year civil war. The largest species remaining are small antelopes known as duikers, with nocturnal tree pangolins being another interesting one to try to spot (good luck). Of the many birds endemic to Angola, this region's most unusual and beautiful is the red-crested turaco, which is quite common in this region, and is the national bird.\nClimate.\nAs with most of Southwest Angola, the climate is relatively dry, though not nearly as arid as the Namib desert.\nGet in.\nYou will need your own 4x4 vehicle to drive here from Lubango or Namibe, or you will need to go with a tour agency. \nSee.\nThe mountain ranges of most interest to 4x4 travelers are the Serra da Chela and the Serra da Leba. The Tundavala Gap in the latter is where you'll find one of the most beautiful views in the whole country.\nThe is a beautiful but fairly inaccessible mountain range in the Great Escarpment. The easiest way to get into these mountains is from Lubango or Namibe, on the road between Capangombe to Humpata.\nThe is also near Lubango, with opportunities for hiking, and including the really spectacular.\nDo.\n4x4 drives and hiking are really all there is to do, and you're on your own!\nEat &amp; drink.\nThere may be roadside street vendors near the villages on either side of the mountains.\nSleep.\nAs with much of Angola, there are not tourist facilities of any kind in these mountains. You can daytrip from Lubango and Namibe, or try your hand at wilderness camping.\nStay safe.\nCertainly don't go alone. If you are, say, hiking, and get hurt, you could die. There will be no one to help you get back to town, and the nearest reputable medical facilities are in South Africa.\nGo next.\nPresumably any visitors here are interested in the wilderness areas of Angola. The most obvious other backcountry sights in this part of the country lie to the west and south of Namibe in the desert."}
{"id": "153809", "revid": "2350368", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153809", "title": "Mussulo Island", "text": "Mussulo Island is just outside Luanda, separated (mostly) from the mainland by the Baie de Mussulo. While actually a peninsula, connected to the mainland by a narrow sandy spit, it is only accessible by boat. It's a popular escape from the Luandan chaos \u2014 clean paradisaical beaches with decent tourist facilities. Because it is popular with wealthy Luandans and expats, the restaurants, bars, and accommodations are expensive.\nGet in.\nMany boats from different locations in Luanda depart daily, with more traffic on weekends. More expensive options are faster, more reliable, and safer.\nGet around.\nMost movement on Mussulo is either on foot or by boat. The sandy terrain and absence of a conventional public-transport network mean that visitors usually remain near their chosen beach, restaurant or resort. Walking is easy for short distances, although loose sand, strong sun and the peninsula\u2019s length make longer journeys tiring. \nLocal boat taxis and privately hired motorboats can carry passengers between beaches, resorts and mainland piers. Some accommodations arrange their own transfers; for example, Mussulito Resort provides transport from Kanawa pier. Confirm the pickup location, fare and return time before boarding, particularly late in the afternoon. \nAlthough it is reportedly possible to reach the southern sandspit by four-wheel-drive vehicle at low tide, boat travel remains the normal and more practical means of access. Visitors unfamiliar with tides and local tracks should not attempt the overland approach independently. \nWeekends are much busier than weekdays, and boats, beach clubs and popular resorts may become crowded. Arrange return transport in advance rather than assuming that a boat will remain available after sunset.\nSee.\nNothing to see here other than the beaches! The Atlantic side is not very safe for swimming, so virtually everything is bay-facing.\nDo.\nBecause this is one of the most developed vacation spots in the country, there are plenty of water sports options: diving, snorkeling, windsurfing, water skiing.\nBuy.\nShopping on Mussulo is limited and mainly aimed at beach visitors. Resorts, bars and informal vendors may sell bottled water, soft drinks, snacks and a few basic beach necessities, but selection is inconsistent. Purchase sunscreen, medicines, toiletries and other important supplies in Luanda, Benfica or Talatona before crossing. \nCarry Angolan kwanza in small denominations. Some established resorts accept cards or Multicaixa, but small vendors and boat operators may require cash. Do not depend on finding an ATM after reaching the peninsula. \nFresh fish may occasionally be bought directly from local fishers, although visitors without cooking facilities will find it easier to eat at a beach restaurant or resort.\nEat.\nMost dining options are attached to beach clubs, resorts and guesthouses. Expect relaxed seaside meals rather than a large selection of independent restaurants. Grilled fish, prawns and other seafood are common, alongside Angolan dishes, rice, beans, salads, grilled meat and simple international meals.\nPopular resorts have their own restaurants, allowing day visitors to combine lunch with beach access or water activities. Mussulito Resort, Resort Madeirense, NAW Beach Resort and several smaller properties provide on-site dining.\nService can be slow during busy weekends, so treat lunch as part of the leisurely Mussulo experience rather than something to rush. Reservations are sensible for larger groups or visits on public holidays. Those going to quieter beaches should bring snacks or a packed meal, as food may not be available away from the main resorts.\nDrink.\nBeach bars and resort restaurants serve bottled water, soft drinks, fruit juices, beer, wine, spirits and cocktails. Sunset drinks overlooking the sheltered bay are a popular way to finish a day trip before returning to the mainland. \nUse sealed bottled or properly treated water rather than drinking untreated tap water. Carry more water than you expect to need, particularly when walking between beaches or visiting during hot weather. \nImported alcohol and drinks at higher-end beach clubs can be expensive. Ask whether beverages are included in any day-use or accommodation package, and carry cash for smaller establishments. Avoid excessive drinking before a boat crossing or water-sports activity.\nSleep.\nAccommodation on Mussulo is predominantly resort-style and is generally more expensive than ordinary lodging elsewhere in Angola. Options range from suites and apartments to private bungalows with swimming pools, restaurants and direct beach access. Book ahead for weekends and holidays, and ask whether the quoted rate includes boat transfers, breakfast or use of beach facilities. \nSome Mussulo properties rely primarily on telephone, WhatsApp or social-media reservations rather than international booking platforms. Contact the accommodation directly to confirm that it is operating, obtain current rates and arrange the boat journey. Ask about electricity, Wi-Fi, hot water and payment requirements before sending a deposit. Independent wild camping is not recommended. Much of the shoreline is privately occupied, and campers would need to arrange drinking water, sanitation, security and return transport without dependable public services. \nMussulito\u2019s location, accommodation types, facilities and Kanawa-pier transfer are listed by Visit Luanda, while the resort\u2019s social profile publishes its direct reservation numbers. \nResort O Madeirense\u2019s official site gives its Mussulo location and direct telephone number; its mapped coordinates and accommodation facilities are corroborated by the local accommodation directory. \nNAW\u2019s mapped location comes from Visit Luanda, while its current official social account publishes the direct reservation number. The resort is described as a 13-room beachfront property with a restaurant and swimming pool. \nMacoco\u2019s official website provides its current telephone numbers, reservation email, accommodation details, transfer instructions, facilities and published rates; its embedded map supplies the coordinates. \nConnect.\nMobile-phone reception is generally available near the more developed parts of Mussulo but may become weak or intermittent along quieter stretches. Unitel has Angola\u2019s largest network and broad coverage in Luanda and major tourism areas, while Africell and Movicel are alternatives. Buy and register a SIM card on the mainland before visiting. \nSome resorts provide Wi-Fi, but speed and reliability vary. Mobile data is usually a better backup than relying entirely on accommodation Wi-Fi. Download maps, booking information and boat-contact details before crossing, and carry a power bank. \nWhatsApp is widely useful for communicating with resorts, drivers and boat operators. Keep the telephone number of your accommodation and skipper available offline, especially when arranging the return journey."}
{"id": "153817", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153817", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/Robben Island steamer wreck", "text": "The dive site Robben Island steamer wreck is a recent offshore wreck about 0.8 nautical miles off the south-western side of Robben Island in the approaches to Table Bay, near Cape Town in the Western Cape Province of South Africa. The vessel was identified as the \"Gilia\" by Henderson and Graham, and is featured in their book \"Wood, Iron and Steel\".\nUnderstand.\nThe wreck is not marked on the navigational charts, and there is no known record of a wreck at this position, but there are many wrecks in Table Bay for which the position is not accurately recorded. The \"Gilia\" was built by Hall, Russell and Co of Aberdeen, Scotland, in 1946 for the South African fishing company Irvin and Johnson. The vessel is a steam powered side trawler of 53.7m and 515 tons, powered by a triple expansion steam engine and a single coal fired boiler. It was scuttled on 24 November 1969.\n S33\u00b049.886', E018\u00b021.524' \u2014 Approximate centre of wreck\nPosition.\nThis site is in a Restricted Area in the Robben Island Marine Protected Area since 2019. A permit is required.\nDepth.\nMaximum depth is about 38\u00a0m on the adjacent sand, and the top of the bow is at about 28\u00a0m. (Average depth of a dive is likely to be about 34\u00a0m.)\nVisibility.\nVisibility is likely to be similar to other parts of Table Bay, and is likely to be best after prolonged or strong South Easterly wind, which is more likely in summer. Visibility at depth may vary significantly from near the surface.\nTopography.\nWreck of a steel steam powered side trawler, spotted on a geological survey, but only identified years later and surveyed on high resulution sonar by Wreckless Marine. The wreckage is approximately 48\u00a0m long, 9\u00a0m beam, lying roughly east-west true, with the bow to the east. The hull lies on a fairly flat sand bottom, canted about 30\u00b0 to port. The ship has a slightly flared plate bow, with very little bow overhang, and square hawse openings. No anchors have been seen yet. There is a winch on the forecastle deck, and a small amount of railing at the bow, The forecastle seems a moderate size and has no bulwarks. The vessel has a cruiser stern, with a fairly long raised quarterdeck, terminating just aft of the boiler. The hull plating appears smooth, and without visible riveting or laps at the joints. There is a single riveted scotch boiler, about 4\u00a0m long, maybe 3\u00a0m diameter, fairly intact, but the stack ducting is completely gone, with fire tubes exposed forward. number of fireboxes has not been reported. There are some heavy accessories on top of the boiler, possibly including the safety valve. There are no reports on the engine, but it may be inside the remaining hull structure aft of the boiler. The deck plating is severely wasted on the quarterdeck, but in better condition on the forecastle. A \"Shires\" ceramic toilet bowl is reported from the forecastle of a model typical of the 1950s to 1960s. Some plating lies on the bottom on both sides of the hull, which could be collapsed hull side plating. The rudder is gone, but the point where the stock would exit the hull is visible. The ship had a single propeller, probably four bladed, possibly cast iron, with broken blades, in a conventional vertical sternpost.\nGeology:\nSand bottom in a gap between areas of reef, probably pre-Cambrian sedimentary rock of the Tygerberg formation.\nConditions.\nThe site is exposed to westerly swells so should be dived in fairly low swells, and may be good after a strong south easterly wind, but is very exposed to wind from all directions. If a strong south-easterly wind develops while diving it would be prudent to leave early as the trip back to Cape Town will be uncomfortable. The site should be at its best in late summer but there are also occasional opportunities in other seasons.\nThis is an area which sometimes has upwellings of cold clear water, caused by strong south easterly winds, resulting in good but cold diving conditions, usually followed by a plankton bloom after a day of sunshine. The site is fairly deep and visibility at depth may be much better than at the surface after a plankton bloom.\nGet in.\nAccess is by boat, either from the V&amp;A Waterfront, or from Granger Bay. \nThe site is about 9.2\u00a0km from the Oceana Power Boat Club slipway at Granger Bay, or about 12\u00a0km from the V&amp;A Waterfront.\nThere are day charter boats which can take a diver to any of the dive sites in the region, but most operators only work from a limited number of launch sites, which limits the dive sites they visit. Visits to a dive site also depend on the weather and are generally not predictable or bookable more than two to four days in advance. See the listing for Cape Peninsula and False Bay boat dive charters and the associated services directory for contact details.\nSee.\nMarine life.\nLight encrustation of sponges and soft corals.\nFeatures.\nThe low level of marine growth leaves the wreck relatively accessible for investigating the wreckage. No penetrations have been reported yet, but there is a fair amount of the forecastle and stern which may be accessible.\nPhotography.\nThe wreck is not very heavily encrusted with marine organisms, so not much of a destination for macro-photography. The visibility will determine whether scenic photos of the wreckage are an option. A wide angle or fish-eye lens is likely to be a good choice most days. The site is fairly deep and likely to be quite dark. Artificial lighting may be necessary even for monochrome.\nSuggested routes.\nThe wreck is fairly small, but the available bottom time is not long, so stay on the main part of the wreck.\nStay safe.\nHazards.\nThe interior of the wreck has not been dived, but based on the size of the vessel it is likely to be tight, and may be silty. Follow wreck penetration procedures if you enter. The wreck is deep enough that nitrogen narcosis may affect most divers using air or nitrox.\nSkills.\nDeep diving skills are recommended, and the ability to deploy a DSMB in case you don't get back to the shotline. Ascents without a marker could get you lost if there is much wind and a current.\nEquipment.\nNitrox is recommended for a reasonable bottom time, and a DSMB in case of ascent away from the shotline in wind or current.\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.000000,-33.750000],[18.400000,-33.750000],\n[18.401000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.750000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.202000,-33.793000],[18.362000,-33.793000],\n[18.362000,-33.900000],[18.202000,-33.900000],[18.20200,-33.793000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of dive sites in the Robben Island area\nOther regional dive sites:"}
{"id": "153832", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153832", "title": "Steam power", "text": "\"Steam power is a travel topic.\"\nSteam power was the driving force for much of the Industrial Revolution in the United Kingdom and United States. It brought travel around the world in eighty days within reach of commercial steamship and rail passengers by the late 1800s, while driving the expansion of both transport and industrialization in many parts of the world.\nUnderstand.\nWhile pilgrimages and educational journeys such as the Grand Tour were established before the Age of Steam, it was steam vehicles which made travel a pleasure, and made recreational tourism possible, allowing common people to visit nearby cities and resorts, the middle class to cross the continent, and the wealthiest to travel around the world. The Grand Old Hotels usually trace their history to the Age of Steam.\nMost reciprocating steam engines were supplanted by internal combustion engines or electric motors during the 20th century, especially in the decades following World War II. The number of steam trains dropped precipitously, due both to widespread dieselization or electrification of existing rail service and replacement of rail travel by highway travel. Steam turbines remain in common use for a few applications, such as electric power generation. Steam locomotives were held in reserve even in Western countries for a long time due to their ability to run on practically any fuel, but many of those have been sold to enthusiasts or scrapped in the 2000s and 2010s.\nSteam railways.\nWhile steam is seen with nostalgia or even longing for the \"good old days\" in places where the last steam service happened some decades past, many developing or emerging economies see the continued existence of any steam locomotives as \"backwards\" and an embarrassment. West Germany had a next-to-total ban of steam on mainline after the last official steam locomotives were withdrawn. Similar attitudes prevail today in some countries. That said, on marginal or otherwise abandoned lines, steam is still frequently to be seen and there is sometimes even a surcharge on tickets when steam locomotives operate compared to \"regular\" diesel trains.\nSteamers, ships and boats.\nBefore the widespread adoption of commercial air travel in the post-World War II era, mighty ocean liners plied the seas. The Royal Mail Ships of the RMS Titanic era, vying to transport the millionaires of the day, competed aggressively on both speed and luxury.\nOn inland rivers such as the Mississippi, the distinctive paddle wheel steamboat was once a common sight. A few still operate either as historic restorations or as replicas, imitations of varying accuracy.\nEngland.\nA small number of small steam powered boats continue to operate on Windermere in the English Lake District.\nFinland.\nAs the country has extensive beautiful archipelagos, there are many tour boats, especially in the lakes, but also some on the coasts. Many of these are former steamships, built to resemble them, or still running on steam (although coal may have been replaced with diesel oil). One of the latter, S/S \"Ukkopekka\", cruised between Turku and Naantali until 2024. S/S \"Bore\", one of the first roll-on cruise ferries on the route to Sweden, was built in 1960 as one of the last Nordic steamships \u2013 for comfort: no shaking from the engines. She is now a hostel, restaurant and event venue in Turku (too much wood in the interior design for the latest IMO regulations).\nSweden.\nMany of Sweden's steamships have been converted to diesel power. Some original steamships tour the Stockholm archipelago and G\u00f6ta kanal.\nStatic steam engines.\nThe earliest use of steam power for industry was pumping (originally from mines), but large steam engines later become the motive power for all manner of industrial machinery, from textiles, to water supply. A few cities (including Otaru Japan, Vancouver Canada and Saint Helier Jersey) purport to operate a steam-powered clock \u2013 or a clock actuating a steam-powered whistle \u2013 as a local landmark in some central point in the village."}
{"id": "153833", "revid": "38175", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=153833", "title": "J\u00e4rvs\u00f6", "text": "J\u00e4rvs\u00f6 is a tourist town in H\u00e4lsingland known for its ski slope, J\u00e4rvs\u00f6backen, and for its zoo.\nUnderstand.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nJ\u00e4rvs\u00f6 is reachable by train or bus. Closest regional airport is H\u00e4rjedalen Sveg Airport[dead link] () which has two daily connections with Stockholm.\nTrain service operated by national operator SJ who have several daily departures from Stockholm and local services operated by X-trafik between G\u00e4vle and Ljusdal via J\u00e4rvs\u00f6."}
