{"id": "154255", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=154255", "title": "Abadi\u00e2nia", "text": "Abadi\u00e2nia is a municipality in the state of Goi\u00e1s, Brazil. It is home to a famous Brazilian medium and \"psychic surgeon\" \u2014 Jo\u00e3o de Deus.\nUnderstand.\nThe life of the town revolves around the work of the medium Joao De Deus. He does healing from Wednesday to Friday in the Casa of Don Ignazio. Every day up to thousands of people come to visit. The area near the Casa is full of pousadas, homes for rent and some restaurants. All the Pousadas serve food as well.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nYou can get a taxi or an Uber from Brasilia international airport. It is a 1 1/2-hour drive.\nBuy.\nIn the road on the way to the Casa of Don Ignacio there are several shops selling crystals.\nBanks.\nThere is only one ATM of Bradescu Bank that dispenses cash. It tends to run out of cash by the end of the week, but is recharged on Tuesday. There is also an ATM in Pousada San Raffael but it charges high fees, better to avoid.\nStay safe.\nAbadi\u00e2nia is a safe place for most travelers.\nDozens of stray dogs live there, the majority of them having contagious infections. Do not hug/cuddle the dogs or take them as your pet dog."}
{"id": "154260", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=154260", "title": "Velestino", "text": "Velestino, Agios Georgios Feron and Chloe are in Magnesia region.\nUnderstand.\nVelstino is situated at 120 metres (390 ft) elevation on a hillside, at the southeastern end of the Thessalian Plain. It is 17 km (11 mi) west of Volos and 40 km southeast of Larissa. The Greek writer and revolutionary Rigas Feraios was born in Velestino in 1757.\nGet in.\nVelestino has a train station on the local line from Larissa to Volos. \nThe Motorway 1 (Athens - Larissa - Thessaloniki) passes east of the town.\nEat.\nVelestino and Agios Georgios Ferron are famous for their grill houses. Many people from Volos go to the grill houses in this area."}
{"id": "154261", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=154261", "title": "Sir Bani Yas Island", "text": "Sir Bani Yas Island is in Abu Dhabi.\nUnderstand.\nSir Bani Yas Island is 170 km southwest of the city of Abu Dhabi and 9 km offshore from Marsa Jebel Dhanna. The island is the UAE's largest naturally-occurring one, at 17 1/2 km from north to south and 9 km from east to west. Sir Bani Yas plays host to a wildlife reserve whose area is over 87 km2. Many varieties of animals, birds and plants live on the island, and in the water, there are dolphins and turtles.\nGet in.\nTake the E11 in the direction of Ruwais, roughly a 3-hour drive. Follow signs for a turnoff to Marsa Jebel Dhanna. From there, a water taxi to the island takes 20 minutes. If you are staying in one of Anantara's resorts on the island, the water taxi is complimentary, but you should contact crome@anantara.com and provide your reservation number to have a seat reserved for you.\nYou could also take a limo from Abu Dhabi city or Dubai or a chartered plane from Abu Dhabi city, if you really have money to burn.\nSee and do.\nThe stars of the wildlife preserve are the flora and fauna, and a major attraction, therefore, is to go on a safari. However, you can also experience the island's hilly terrain on a bicycle, hike, snorkel, kayak, or do archery.\nEat and drink.\nThe resorts have restaurants on the premises. Breakfast is complimentary for guests, but you will have to pay extra for other meals.\nSleep.\nAnantara operates all three accommodations on the island. They are all called resorts and priced accordingly."}
{"id": "154270", "revid": "2430204", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=154270", "title": "Gold Coast/Broadbeach", "text": "Broadbeach is a beachside district in the city of Gold Coast in the south of Queensland in Australia. It is directly to the south of the Surfers Paradise suburb. This Wikivoyage article covers the suburbs of Broadbeach and Broadbeach Waters.\nGet in.\nTake Bus 777 from Gold Coast Airport, which will take you to Broadbeach South Tram Station in 30 minutes.\nA taxi from the airport will be slightly shorter (25 minutes) and cost $50."}
{"id": "154274", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=154274", "title": "Purcell", "text": "Purcell is a town in Oklahoma.\nUnderstand.\nPurcell was founded in 1894, as a town in the Chickasaw Nation of the Indian Territory, becoming the seat McClain County in 1907 when Oklahoma became a state.\nPurcell is known as the \"Quarterhorse Capital of the World.\" \nMost travelers on I-35 see Purcell as a stop for refreshment but those who venture into the old downtown can experience a quaint downtown with brick streets, antique stores and good quality mom &amp; pop cafes.\nGet in.\nPurcell is a stop on the Amtrak Heartland Flyer which travels between Oklahoma City and Fort Worth on a daily round-trip schedule. \nThe town is a short drive south from the OKC metro area via I-35.\nEat.\nThere are lots of fast food chain restaurants in town."}
{"id": "154307", "revid": "113846", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=154307", "title": "Walls", "text": " \"Not to be confused with South Walls, an Orkney island linked by causeway to Hoy\" \nWalls or Waas (Old Norse \"Vagar\" \"Sheltered Bays\") is a small fishing village on the west Mainland of the Shetland Islands. The main reasons to come are the Neolithic \"Temple\" and to take the ferry to Foula, or the ferry from West Burrafirth to Papa Stour.\nGet in.\nBy road from Lerwick it's 19 miles (30 km). Turn off A970 onto A971 at Lerwick-Tingwall airport.\nBus 9 runs five times M-F from Lerwick to Walls, taking 50 min via Whiteness and Hellister.\n is half a mile south of the main village. The BK Marine ferry to Foula sails twice a week (Tu and Th), foot passengers only, taking 2 hours. It only carries 12 so reservations are essential on +44 1595 840208. A day trip is not possible by ferry.\n, 20 miles north of Walls (and even more weary miles from anywhere else), is the ferry pier for Papa Stour. This ferry runs twice on W F and Sa, taking 45 min, so a day trip gives you five hours on the island. Sunday there's only one sailing. Until April 2025, the return fare is \u00a32.80 per adult, child or conc \u00a30.80.\nUntil 2019 there were flights between Lerwick-Tingwall and Papa Stour. It's not known when or if these might resume.\n \"Oot bewast da Horn o Papa, Rowin Foula doon! Owir a hidden piece o water, Rowin Foula doon!\"\n - for the best fishing off Papa Stour, you had to row into the Atlantic until the cliffs of Foula dropped out of sight. They're 1200 ft high and can be seen for 40 miles, and Foula itself is 20-some miles offshore, so that's 60 miles out. The boats were frail open \"sixareens\" and a trip took 3 days, with two trips a week. \nGet around.\nYou need wheels to reach Staneydale Temple, five miles east of Walls, or West Burrafirth for the ferry to Papa Stour.\nBuy.\nThe convenience store, next to the bakery, is open M-Sa 9AM-6PM, Su noon-4:30PM.\nEat.\nWaas Bakery in the village does nice pastries.\nDrink.\nBring your own. Burrastow House serves alcohol to residents but doesn't have a public bar.\nConnect.\nAs of Sep 2024, there is no signal from any mobile carrier in the vicinity of Walls or West Burrafirth, or on Papa Stour."}
